WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actor and host

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.203
you know from his work on shows

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:06.305
like "Brooklyn Nine Nine"
and "America's Got Talent".

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:09.375
His new memoir, "Tough:
My Journey to True Power,"

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:10.443
is out Tuesday.

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.477
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:13.179
our friend, Terry Crews,
everybody.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.815
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:21.354 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:26.859 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:31.297 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:32.598
-Wow!

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:35.468
-So nice to have you back,
in the studio, no less!

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:37.337
-Real people!
-Real people.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:40.139
-Real people. I love real
people. I love you!

00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:41.107
-They are very nice.

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:42.174
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:44.811
And real peope love you.
We missed you.

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:48.281
We had a "Brooklyn Nine Nine"
finale show.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:49.749
You had obviously
filmed the finale

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:51.017
months before we did this,

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:52.151
but we had
the gang back together.

00:00:52.151 --> 00:00:54.286
You weren't here,
but we went out of our way

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:55.888
to make sure
you were represented.

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:57.924
And were you happy
with the cardboard cutout

00:00:57.924 --> 00:00:58.957
we used for you?

00:00:58.957 --> 00:01:00.593
-I saw it. I loved it.

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:02.728
The pecs popped really
wonderful.

00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:05.365
-You can see
that there's no technology.

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:06.666
This is not CGI.

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:08.134
-This is all real.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:10.636
This is it!

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:13.006
-But we got the finest
the finest technicians on Earth

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:15.608
to see if they could replicate
this on a cardboard cutout.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:16.509
Let's take a look.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.610
We do have a cutout Terry,

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:22.882
and we're very happy with
the technology that we've used

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:25.018
to make his pecs go.

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:27.553
-Ooh!
-Yeah, it's really good.

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:29.188
Really good.
-Yeah.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.391
-You know, the thing is, every
year it gets better and better,

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:33.993
because they kind of
droop a little more.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:36.162
-Right.
Oh, that's interesting.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:37.697
-In a minute, I'm gonna
hit myself in the face.

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.032
It's gonna be like,
oh, oh, oh.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:40.800
-The best thing is,
every single one

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.169
of your cast mates said, "I want
it, I want it, I want it."

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:45.238
And then they just
left it in the hallway

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.506
and we had to get rid of it,

00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:47.606
because every time
I walked to my dressing room,

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:48.741
I'd turn on the lights

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:49.742
and it would scare
the [bleep] out of me.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.010
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.845
-You know, I have a statue
of myself in my house.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:53.979
-Who doesn't?
-I know!

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:55.648
Who doesn't have a statue?

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:58.651
And my wife would go in and it
would scare her every morning.

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:00.453
She was like, "You've got to
get this sucker out of here."

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.455
-How long have you had
a statue of yourself?

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:03.989
-It was for fittings

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:06.259
and stuff like that,
for the show.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.260
So I was out
and they would fit the thing,

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.396
and then I fell in love
with it and I kept it.

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.632
[ Laughing ]

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:15.868
-So it sounds like a mannequin
that you're calling a statue.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:19.438
-Well.
-Okay.

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:20.806
So was it hard?

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:22.908
You know, I can only imagine
what it was like

00:02:22.908 --> 00:02:24.410
the last day of shooting
on a show like that

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.577
that had such a wonderful cast.

00:02:25.577 --> 00:02:27.146
You all seemed genuinely close.

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:31.117
-I still get emotional
about this, because, for one,

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:33.619
you know, I spent more time
with that cast

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:34.988
than I did my own family.

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:36.589
And, you know...

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.558
-But your family got to be
with the statue.

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:40.192
It wasn't like they had no one.
-Yeah.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:41.227
You know what I mean?

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:44.297
It was raising my kids.

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:46.599
But, you know,
but we got the chance

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:48.567
to end it the way we wanted.

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:51.371
I mean, it's totally bittersweet
because it was so sad.

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:53.906
But at the same time, like,
we got an ending.

00:02:53.906 --> 00:02:57.210
There's so many shows that just
cut off and it's a wrap.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.612
And I think we ended it
the perfect way.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:00.713
It was perfect.
-Well, congratulations.

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:03.015
It was a really lovely one.

00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:04.317
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:07.687
So this is your second memoir.
-Yes.

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:09.188
-I can imagine
early in your career,

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:11.190
you might not have thought you
would have one memoir in you,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.458
and now you've got two.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:14.894
-Well, first of all,
when I did my first one in 2014,

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.128
it was called "Manhood".

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:18.397
And I was like, "This is it.
This is the one.

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:22.068
And then more life happened
and more questions happened

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.302
and people kept, you know --

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:25.270
The first thing I brought up
in that first book

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.472
was toxic masculinity.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:28.340
And people were like,
"What is that?"

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.543
And then the Academy Awards
happened,

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:33.178
and you start to understand,

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:34.780
and you start
to see all these examples.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.283
And I got more and more
questions, and I said,

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:38.851
"It's time for another book,"

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:41.154
because I wasn't
always the nice guy

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:43.623
you see in front of you
right now, Seth.

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:47.193
There were times that
I had extreme rage,

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:49.762
and it was so wild,
because there had been

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.531
a lot of people who've been
knocked out by me before.

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:55.868
And what was so nuts is
that my wife was literally like,

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:57.536
"Hey, you got to promise me

00:03:57.536 --> 00:04:01.474
that you will never react
in violence, ever again."

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:04.476
And I was like, "Oh, come on.
I got to do this. I'm a man."

00:04:04.476 --> 00:04:05.945
And she's like, "No. No."

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:09.348
And the time that I didn't,
it actually saved my life,

00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:11.617
which was so incredible.

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:13.285
-That's
an amazing thing to hear.

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:16.422
And I can only imagine how
easy it was to fall into that.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:18.691
Obviously, you had a
professional football career

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:19.859
before you
found your way into acting.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:22.962
That's, you know,
an inherently violent sport.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:25.898
I've heard you say
you did you did not have

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:27.533
an ego to match your skills.

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:28.834
Can you explain exactly...?

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:30.636
-Well, I wasn't that good
a football player.

00:04:30.636 --> 00:04:32.004
-Yeah.
-Okay, let's just...

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:33.305
-You must've been good
at level...

00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:36.375
When did you realize
it was above your, like --

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:37.877
Was it the pros
where you were like --

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.178
-No, I went all the way.

00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:42.882
You got to understand,
you can go a long way in the NFL

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:45.418
with a bad parent
and lots of rage.

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:48.487
It'll get you really far,
because you take a lot of pain.

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:50.322
-That's not in
any of the commercials.

00:04:50.322 --> 00:04:52.525
-No, it's not. It's not.

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:53.792
But you will.

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:56.161
You'll go, because it's
one of those things where

00:04:56.161 --> 00:04:57.963
they need someone who --

00:04:57.963 --> 00:05:00.333
First of all,
I also needed approval,

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:02.201
so coaches and different things,

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:05.405
and that was my way
to get approval from people,

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:06.773
in playing ball
and all this stuff.

00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:08.241
But one thing I realized is that

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.976
I actually did not like
football at all.

00:05:10.976 --> 00:05:13.279
-Did you have a lights on moment
when that happened for you?

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:16.482
-Yes, I was trying out
for the 49ers.

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:19.051
It was my -- like, I was
on six teams in seven years.

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:22.055
So the seventh team
I was trying out for,

00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:25.258
the coach threw the ball at me
and it dislocated my finger.

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:27.360
My finger was sitting there
like a L and I was like,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.662
"I don't like this anymore.

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.431
Why am I playing?
I don't like it."

00:05:32.431 --> 00:05:35.034
And you know what I realized?
This was the thing that hit me.

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:40.273
I realized I just liked playing
outside all day with my friends.

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:41.841
That was it.

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:43.542
And once I started to do that

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:44.944
and substitute it
with all this stuff,

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:47.880
I was like, "I was getting hit
all these years,

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.114
and I didn't need to do that."

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:51.250
I was like, "Man, we could
just go play badminton."

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.852
-Yeah.
-And it was all good.

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:56.455
I had the breakthrough you had
as a Pop Warner football player.

00:05:56.455 --> 00:05:57.923
-I remember.
-[ Laughing ]

00:05:57.923 --> 00:06:00.392
-Yeah, I remember I was
on a Pop Warner football team

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.061
and we were in,
like, the state finals.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:04.130
And if we won,
we'd go to regionals,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.932
and I was sitting on the bench,
where I spent a lot of my time,

00:06:06.932 --> 00:06:09.935
and our team fumbled
and we lost.

00:06:09.935 --> 00:06:14.139
And I realized I hated football,
'cause I was so happy.

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:16.175
Like, our whole team was
upset on the bus.

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:19.211
And I was like,
"Oh, I think I hate this."

00:06:19.211 --> 00:06:20.913
[ Laughter ]
Hey, this was --

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:21.981
I know you're also an artist.

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:23.716
We've talked about this
on the show.

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:27.519
You wrote and illustrated
one of my kid's favorite books,

00:06:27.519 --> 00:06:29.555
and you used to do
portraits of players.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:30.623
So this was during your
playing days?

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:31.824
-That's right.
-And you would sort of

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:33.625
supplement your income
with this?

00:06:33.625 --> 00:06:36.796
-What I used to do is,
because I got cut a lot, okay?

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:39.732
So I would go back
in the locker rooms

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:42.834
and ask the players if they
wanted their portraits painted,

00:06:42.834 --> 00:06:45.337
because they had just
as big an ego as I did.

00:06:45.337 --> 00:06:48.474
I was like, "Yo, I'm going
to make you look so amazing.

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:49.809
You're going to look great."

00:06:49.809 --> 00:06:52.144
"Yeah, man. I want that, man.
Yeah, boy."

00:06:52.144 --> 00:06:54.914
And I would get money
for these paintings

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:56.582
and I would do like,
three or four paintings

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:59.285
in the offseason, get like
three or four grand apiece,

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:02.088
and it would help me
survive with my family

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:04.056
and get me to the next team.

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:06.191
Because I was in art.

00:07:06.191 --> 00:07:08.394
I had an art scholarship before
I had a football scholarship.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:11.264
But now it's so good
to actually be

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:13.098
and live this artistic life.

00:07:13.098 --> 00:07:14.867
This is what
I always wanted, you know?

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:16.068
-Well, it's nice,
and now, of course,

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:18.504
you're on a show
that is surrounded by talent

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:19.872
and all different talents.

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.474
And when Simon Cowell was here,
I asked him, you know,

00:07:22.474 --> 00:07:25.010
do people on the street,
do people in public

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.680
ever go up to you and just
start doing their talent

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.849
in hopes that they'll get like
a little, free judgment?

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:31.184
-[ Sighs ]
-That ever happen for you?

00:07:31.184 --> 00:07:32.484
-Yes.

00:07:32.484 --> 00:07:34.820
First of all, I was on a plane
and the pilot comes out.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.988
He's like, "I love
'America's Got Talent'!

00:07:36.988 --> 00:07:39.725
And, you know, first of all,
there's a bug and a rat

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:41.027
and they go into a bar --"

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:43.929
And you're like, "Oh, man.
He's doing his steal!"

00:07:43.929 --> 00:07:46.265
He's a stand-up comedian pilot!

00:07:46.265 --> 00:07:49.568
And I was like, "But who's
flying the plane, man?"

00:07:49.568 --> 00:07:52.838
I was like, "But that's funny."

00:07:52.838 --> 00:07:56.074
And, you know, I'm like,
"Ha ha ha, let's fly the plane."

00:07:56.074 --> 00:07:58.243
And it's going. It's in the air.
-Yeah.

00:07:58.243 --> 00:08:00.679
-And he's just doing his spiel
and you've got to laugh.

00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:01.847
It's a captive audience.

00:08:01.847 --> 00:08:03.749
-Yeah, 'cause then you're
like, of the two pilots,

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:05.885
you want at least
one of their main talents

00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:09.288
to be flying a plane, you know?
-Exactly.

00:08:09.288 --> 00:08:10.923
-Hey, congratulations
on the book.

00:08:10.923 --> 00:08:11.957
-Oh.

00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:12.958
-It's always a delight
to see you.

00:08:12.958 --> 00:08:14.193
Terry Crews, everybody!

